You've just said it yourself, but I didn't quite realise myself where I
was coming from until you said it: Priestess. Those fancy pointies
you've just shown are the kind of thing I would expect a
priest/priestess/druid/shaman/other mystic-type to use. What's more, I
would expect the athames to be used more for cermemonial/ sacrificial/
invoking the gods/spirits stuff than actual spellcasting. I've a
feeling your priestess may even agree with this last statement. I also
suspect she'd identify more with nethack's priest class than the wizard
class, who are (in my imagination at least) long-grey-beard Gandalf/
Merlin types. Wizards are founded in the study and practise of magic,
priests work on sprituality/ mysticism, which isn't quite the same
thing (in most fictional mythos, at least, I can't speak for your
priestess). Granted, there is a great deal of cultural and mythological
cross-pollination between wizards/witches/priests/shamans, but they are
obviously distinct enough to warrant two seperate classes in Our Game.

So yeah, what I'm saying is, that although a learned wizard would
appreciate and have a certain understanding of the magical/ mystical
properties of an athame, I would expect him to trade it in for a good
old fashioned knobbly magic staff any day. What's more, being able to
wave an athame about and cast spells/ talk to the Gods wouldn't
necessarily enable a person to machine gun daggers three at a time with
a rogue's deadly accuracy. Likewise, a priest(ess) would have a good
deal of understanding of a wizard's lore, but wouldn't feel entirely at
home with it.

Perhaps then in nethack athames and staffs could both be used as
"magical props" to enchace spellcasting, but for different classes of
spell: Things like magic missile and detect food would benefit from a
staff, while more 'clerical' spells like remove curse and turn undead
would do better with an athame.

On a slightly different note, how about athames having special favour
(for all classes) when used in sacrifice? Either the game could track
any corpse where the final blow was delivered by an athame (extra
points if the corpse died ON the altar) and increasing its sac-value
accordingly, or a simple bonus for wielding an athame while carrying
out the actual sacrifice.

